\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation.}
LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes (position bias, verbosity, sycophancy), motivating multi-judge panels; \citet{raina2024judgerobust} quantified robustness gaps under adversarial attacks, and \citet{maloyan2025investigating} demonstrated prompt injection vulnerabilities. Aggregating noisy annotators traces back to \citet{dawid1979maximum}; \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias driving within-family correlations. \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\%, but did not disentangle diversity from individual robustness. \citet{tamas2025benchmarking} benchmark adversarial risks in multi-agent systems but focus on agent coordination; \citet{qian2026collabeval} propose collaborative multi-phase evaluation but assume benign conditions. We test whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation.

\paragraph{Condorcet Jury Theorem.}
\citet{condorcet1785essai} proved that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5; \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended the theorem to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee. The Condorcet Jury Theorem (CJT) explicitly assumes independent, non-adversarial errors; its failure under adaptive adversaries is trivially expected \citep{tramer2018ensemble,kariyappa2019improving,pang2019improving}. We focus on the \emph{panel-size-dependent structure} of this vulnerability: the predictor shift from co-determination at $K{=}3$ to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance at $K{\geq}5$.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity Theory.}
\citet{krogh1995neural} proved that ensemble error equals average member error minus a diversity term; \citet{brown2005managing} showed diversity reduces error only when members maintain sufficiently low bias. In our setting, weaker judges introduce bias large enough to offset diversity benefits. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} found that no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicted ensemble improvement; our $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a $K$-dependent pattern: at $K{=}3$, OLS significance (12/12) attenuates under bootstrap (4/12); at $K{\geq}5$, it survives bootstrap inference (7/12). Recent information-theoretic frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness and Transferability.}
Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving} or diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision but requires coordinating member training, a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection. Our setting differs: adversaries use natural-language manipulation rather than gradient perturbations, and aggregation rules are a controllable design parameter.

\paragraph{BFT and Security Games.}
Classical BFT \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical} tolerates up to $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle that an attacker exploits the single most vulnerable component. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate BFT's independence assumption, motivating our empirical decomposition. \citet{kuai2026independent} propose the Difficulty-Weighted Behavioral Entanglement Index (DBEI), weighting agreement by item difficulty; $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ captures a complementary dimension via pairwise MI, providing an information-theoretic measure of panel redundancy. Mitigation strategies include DBEI-based reweighting, Bayesian calibration, and credibility scoring \citep{ebrahimi2025adversary}.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Evaluation.}
Information-theoretic approaches address statistical validity \citep{robertson2025stepwise,feng2026noisy}, attack costs \citep{kaneko2025bits}, sequential selection \citep{jung2026logarithmic}, and truthfulness \citep{chen2025incentivizing,qiu2026truthfulness}, but assume honest judges. We address adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API; six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with Activation-aware Weight Quantization (AWQ) INT4 on an NVIDIA RTX~6000 Ada (48\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ panels at $K{=}3$, $\binom{15}{5} = 3{,}003$ at $K{=}5$, and $\binom{15}{7} = 6{,}435$ at $K{=}7$, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lllccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Deploy} & \textbf{MMLU} & \textbf{ARC} \\
\midrule
gpt-5.5              & OpenAI    & frontier    & API   & .957 & .983 \\
gemini-3.1-pro-preview & Google  & frontier    & API   & .955 & .987 \\
claude-opus-4-6       & Anthropic & frontier   & API   & .890 & .987 \\
gemini-3.5-flash      & Google    & frontier   & API   & .968 & 1.00$^\dagger$ \\
\addlinespace
gpt-4.1               & OpenAI    & mid-tier   & API   & .837 & .967 \\
claude-sonnet-4-6     & Anthropic & mid-tier   & API   & .810 & .953 \\
\addlinespace
gpt-4.1-mini          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .800 & .963 \\
gpt-4.1-nano          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .633 & .853 \\
claude-3-haiku        & Anthropic & lightweight & API  & .767 & .450 \\
mistral-large (123B)  & Mistral   & lightweight & local & .537 & .533 \\
llama3.1-70b          & Llama     & lightweight & local & .523 & .520 \\
llama3.1-8b           & Llama     & lightweight & local & .543 & .320 \\
qwen2.5-72b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .333 & .410 \\
qwen2.5-32b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .373 & .450 \\
qwen2.5-14b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .253 & .240 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Judge models used in the study (clean accuracy on 300-item samples). API models accessed via OpenRouter (May 2026 snapshots); local models served with vLLM + AWQ INT4 quantization. $^\dagger$\,gemini-3.5-flash achieves perfect ARC accuracy but with 26.3\% format error rate. Tier assignment follows provider capability classification (frontier $>$85\% clean accuracy on both benchmarks, mid-tier 80--85\%, lightweight ${\leq}$80\% or quantized); all six local AWQ INT4 models fall into lightweight, where quantization-induced accuracy reduction conflates genuine capability limitations with quantization damage (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each. All models use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs (temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}).

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types: (1)~\textbf{prompt injection}, adversarial instructions overriding the evaluation prompt \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}; (2)~\textbf{sycophancy}, flattering language exploiting judge preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; (3)~\textbf{score manipulation}, explicit numerical cues anchoring scoring; (4)~\textbf{verbosity bias}, padding with irrelevant detail \citep{zheng2023judging}; (5)~\textbf{position bias}, reordering to exploit positional preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; and (6)~\textbf{authority bias}, fabricated credentials inflating credibility. The 2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks produce 12 conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (details in Appendix~\ref{app:attack_details}).

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Per-judge vulnerability under attack $a$ is the fraction of items where judge $j$ is fooled: $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$, estimated empirically over the item set. The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the most susceptible member of each panel. The panel attack success rate $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ is the fraction of items where at least $t$ judges are simultaneously fooled. Panel diversity is quantified by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), computed from pairwise MI between judges' three-category winner labels (model~A wins, model~B wins, tie) on clean data, using the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow bias correction. The $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}(i,j))$ term measures pairwise alienation: 1 when independent, 0 under full dependence. Under a Gaussian copula this yields $K_{\mathrm{eff}} = K - \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i}\sum_{j \neq i} \rho_{ij}^2$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}; goodness-of-fit rejects the Gaussian copula for 97--100\% of pairs, yet plug-in MI and closed-form rankings agree closely ($\rho{\approx}0.86$; Appendix~\ref{app:copula_gof}). Together, $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link channel) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity channel) serve as the two predictors in the regression framework below.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond majority vote ($\geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$ approvals), we evaluate three alternatives: \emph{accuracy-weighted vote} ($w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$; accept if $\sum_j w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$), \emph{threshold vote} (unanimity, $t{=}K$), and \emph{Bayesian calibration} ($P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$; accept if posterior $> 0.5$).

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To disentangle the contributions of panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values (sensitivity to $\epsilon$ is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}). Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini--Hochberg (BH) correction for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate (FDR) per predictor across the 12 conditions.

Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} decomposes $R^2$ into each predictor's average incremental contribution across all subsets. Generalized additive models (GAMs) with smooth terms test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect direction varies between targeted attacks (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) and shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity, authority bias).

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each appears in 91 panels), inducing correlated residuals. We apply three corrections: clustered standard errors (SE; CR1, 3-position max-SE), mixed-effects models (random judge intercepts), and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, 3-position max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. Cluster counts (13, ${\leq}11$, ${\leq}9$ at $K{=}3,5,7$) fall below the ${\geq}20$ threshold; we use Rademacher weights following \citet{mackinnon2018wild}. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods}).

